{"name":"Sweet Potato Peel","hex":"#917798","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tasting of sugars.","Retaining a portion of sugar."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used as a positive response to good news or information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweet"},{"term":"potato","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The edible starchy tuber of the plant species Solanum tuberosum.","The plant that produces this tuber."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hit very hard; to pummel.","To hit with a thrown or fired potato."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/potato"},{"term":"peel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove the skin or outer covering of.","To remove something from the outer or top layer of."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The skin or outer layer of a fruit, vegetable, etc.","The action of peeling away from a formation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweet potato","blocks":[{"text":"The sweet potato or sweetpotato is a dicotyledonous plant in the morning glory family, Convolvulaceae. Its sizeable, starchy, sweet-tasting, tuberous roots are used as a root vegetable, which is a staple food in parts of the world. Cultivars of the sweet potato have been bred to bear tubers with flesh and skin of various colors. Moreover, the young shoots and leaves are eaten as greens with nutritional composition comparable to spinach. The sweet potato and the potato are only distantly related, both being in the order Solanales. Although darker sweet potatoes are often known as yams in parts of North America, they are even more distant from actual yams, which are monocots in the order Dioscoreales."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Ipomoea_batatas_006.JPG/330px-Ipomoea_batatas_006.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_potato"},{"title":"Sweetness","blocks":[{"text":"Sweetness is a basic taste most commonly perceived when eating foods rich in sugar. Sweet tastes are generally regarded as pleasurable. In addition to sugars like sucrose, many other chemical compounds are sweet, including aldehydes, ketones, and sugar alcohols. Some are sweet at very low concentrations, allowing their use as non-caloric sugar substitutes. Such non-sugar sweeteners include saccharin, aspartame, sucralose and stevia. Other compounds, such as miraculin, may alter perception of sweetness itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Strawberry_shortcake.jpg/330px-Strawberry_shortcake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweetness"},{"title":"Potato","blocks":[{"text":"The potato is a starchy tuberous vegetable native to the Americas that is consumed as a staple food in many parts of the world. Potatoes are underground stem tubers of the plant Solanum tuberosum, a perennial in the nightshade family Solanaceae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Patates.jpg/330px-Patates.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potato"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Genasu_gadde_%28Kannada_%E0%B2%97%E0%B3%86%E0%B2%A3%E0%B2%B8%E0%B3%81_%E0%B2%97%E0%B2%A1%E0%B3%8D%E0%B2%A1%E0%B3%86%29_%2849108875711%29.jpg/500px-Genasu_gadde_%28Kannada_%E0%B2%97%E0%B3%86%E0%B2%A3%E0%B2%B8%E0%B3%81_%E0%B2%97%E0%B2%A1%E0%B3%8D%E0%B2%A1%E0%B3%86%29_%2849108875711%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Genasu_gadde_%28Kannada_%E0%B2%97%E0%B3%86%E0%B2%A3%E0%B2%B8%E0%B3%81_%E0%B2%97%E0%B2%A1%E0%B3%8D%E0%B2%A1%E0%B3%86%29_%2849108875711%29.jpg/1280px-Genasu_